### 3.3. Reading Data From a File

To read data from a file, you can use the `fopen()`, `fread()` and `fclose()` functions provided by C Standard Library. Here is an example of how to read data from a file:
```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    FILE *fp;
    char buffer[1024];
    int bytes_read = 0;

    // Open the file in binary mode
    fp = fopen("data.bin", "rb");

    if (fp == NULL) {
        printf("File could not be opened\n");
        return -1;
    }

    // Read data from the file and store it in buffer
    bytes_read = fread(buffer, 1024, 1, fp);

    if (bytes_read > 0) {
        printf("Data read successfully\n");
        printf("Buffer: %s\n", buffer);
    } else {
        printf("No data read from file\n");
    }

    // Close the file
    fclose(fp);

    return 0;
}
```
This code will read a binary file named `data.bin` and store its content in a buffer of size 1024 bytes. It uses `fopen()` function to open the file in binary mode, `fread()` function to read data from the file and `fclose()` function to close the file. The `fread()` function returns the number of bytes it was able to read, so if this value is greater than 0, then there is some data to be read from the file.

