        float new_coordinate = get_new_coordinate();
        sensors[i].coordinates[0] = new_coordinate;
        sensors[i].coordinates[1] = new_coordinate;
        sensors[i].coordinates[2] = new_coordinate;
    }
}

// Function to free memory allocated for sensor data structures
void free_memory(int num_sensors) {
    if (num_sensors > MAX_SENSORS) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Number of sensors exceeds the maximum allowed\n");
        exit(EXIT_FAILURE);
    }
    for (int i = 0; i < num_sensors; i++) {
        // Free memory allocated for each sensor data structure
        free(sensors[i].coordinates);
        sensors[i].coordinates = NULL;
        free(sensors[i].id);
        sensors[i].id = NULL;
        free(sensors[i].label);
        sensors[i].label = NULL;
    }
}
```
This implementation defines a container to store all sensors in the system, and provides functions to dynamically allocate memory for each sensor data structure. The `allocate_memory` function takes an integer argument representing the number of sensors to be managed by the system and checks if it exceeds the maximum allowed limit (1000). If not, it allocates memory for each sensor data structure and initializes them with random values within specified ranges.

The `initialize_sensor` function takes an integer argument representing the number of sensors to be initialized and checks if it exceeds the maximum allowed limit (1000). If not, it initializes each sensor data structure with random values within specified ranges for coordinates, ID, and label.

The `update_sensor` function takes an integer argument representing the number of sensors whose readings need to be updated and checks if it exceeds the maximum allowed limit (1000). If not, it updates each sensor data structure with new readings obtained from a source external to the system.

The `free_memory` function takes an integer argument representing the number of sensors whose memory needs to be freed and checks if it exceeds the maximum allowed limit (1000). If not, it frees the memory allocated for each sensor data structure.

To test this implementation, you can call the `allocate_memory`, `initialize_sensor`, `update_sensor`, and `free_memory` functions with different integer arguments representing the number of sensors to be managed by the system. For example, you can call `allocate_memory(100)` to allocate memory for 100 sensor data structures, initialize them with random values within specified ranges, update their readings, and then free their memory.

Please note that this implementation assumes that the readings obtained from an external source are in float format and can be used directly to update the sensor data structures. If your readings have a different format or need to be processed before updating the sensor data structures, you may need to modify the `update_sensor` function accordingly.']

