Answer: \section{Use `std::vector` instead of raw C arrays}

C++ has a lot of good stuff built-in for handling dynamic memory allocation, like `std::vector`. You can use these to replace the manual memory management that you have at the moment.

\begin{code}
std::vector<int> sensor_readings(num_sensors);
\end{code}

\section{Simplify initialization of struct members}

Instead of initializing each member of a `struct` separately, you can use aggregate initialization. For example, instead of:

\begin{code}
sensor_reading sr = {0, {'\0'}};
\end{code}

You can write:

\begin{code}
sensor_reading sr = {0, ""};
\end{code}

This is especially useful in your `update_sensor_reading` function, where you have to initialize the entire struct with new values.

\section{Consider using a `std::string` instead of a char array}

A `std::string` is already capable of handling dynamic memory allocation for you, so it's worth considering whether or not you really need a char array here. If you do end up using a `std::string`, you may be able to get rid of the manual memory management functions entirely.

\section{Use `const` where appropriate}

You should use `const` whenever possible, especially when referring to variables that are not going to change. For example:

\begin{code}
void update_sensor_reading(size_t index, int sensor_reading, const char *unique_identifier) { ... }
\end{code}

\section{Consider using `std::map` instead of a custom data structure}

Instead of defining your own data structure for storing the sensor readings and unique identifiers, you could use a pre-defined data structure like `std::map`. This would take care of the memory management for you, and also allow you to look up readings by their unique identifier more efficiently.

\section{Use meaningful variable names}

In your code, there are a few instances where you're using single-letter variables, like `t` and `n`. These are not very descriptive, so it can be difficult to tell what they represent. Consider using more descriptive names, especially in cases where the variable is used multiple times throughout the function or class.

\section{Consider using a `const` reference for parameters}

Instead of passing in a copy of the parameter by value like this:

\begin{code}
void update_sensor_reading(size_t index, int sensor_reading, const char *unique_identifier) { ... }
\end{code}

You can pass it as a `const` reference instead, which will prevent the parameter from being modified by the function. This can be useful if you don't want to modify the original value, and also allows the function to take advantage of RVO (Return Value Optimization) if the parameter is an aggregate type like a struct or a class with only public members:

\begin{code}
void update_sensor_reading(size_t index, int sensor_reading, const std::string& unique_identifier) { ... }
\end{code}

