// Function to safely update a record in the array of records
void update_record(Record* records, int index, long offset, int key, double value) {
    if (index < 0 || index >= num_records) {
        printf("Error: invalid record index %d\n", index);
        return;
    }

    records[index].offset = offset;
    records[index].key = key;
    records[index].value = value;
}

// Function to safely free the memory allocated for an array of records
void free_records(Record* records) {
    if (!records) {
        printf("Error: no records to free\n");
        return;
    }

    free(records);
}

// Function to safely free the memory allocated for a buffer of long offset values
void free_offset_buffer(long* offset_buffer) {
    if (!offset_buffer) {
        printf("Error: no offset buffer to free\n");
        return;
    }

    free(offset_buffer);
}

// Function to safely free the memory allocated for a buffer of int keys
void free_key_buffer(int* key_buffer) {
    if (!key_buffer) {
        printf("Error: no key buffer to free\n");
        return;
    }

    free(key_buffer);
}

// Function to safely free the memory allocated for a buffer of double values
void free_value_buffer(double* value_buffer) {
    if (!value_buffer) {
        printf("Error: no value buffer to free\n");
        return;
    }

    free(value_buffer);
}
```

In this example, we have defined four functions that can be used to safely allocate and initialize memory for an array of records, as well as to update a single record in the array. We have also defined separate functions to safely free the memory allocated for each buffer type.

The `allocate_records` function takes a single argument, which is the number of records to be allocated, and returns a pointer to an array of records. The `update_record` function takes three arguments: a pointer to the array of records, an index into the array, and a new value for the corresponding record.

The `free_records`, `free_offset_buffer`, `free_key_buffer`, and `free_value_buffer` functions take a single argument, which is a pointer to the memory buffer to be freed.

In addition to these functions, we have also defined some utility functions that can be used to check for errors in our memory allocation and deallocation code. These functions include:

* `check_memory_error`: This function checks if there was an error in the most recent call to `malloc` or `calloc`. If there was an error, it prints a message to standard error indicating that there was an error allocating memory.
* `check_free_error`: This function checks if there was an error in the most recent call to `free`. If there was an error, it prints a message to standard error indicating that there was an error deallocating memory.

These utility functions can be used anywhere in our code to check for errors in memory allocation and deallocation. For example:

