This implementation is quite complex and has a lot of redundancy. It also does not handle the case where `max_size` is smaller than the current size of the array, which could lead to memory leaks or data corruption.

A better way to implement this would be to use a single mutex to protect both the reallocation of the arrays and the insertion/removal of elements in the array. This can be done by using a `pthread_mutex_t` object, which is a special kind of lock that can be used to protect critical sections of code from concurrent access.

Here's an example implementation that uses a single mutex to protect the arrays and elements:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <pthread.h>

typedef struct {
    int key;
    void *value;
} Record;

Record *records = NULL;
int max_size = 0;
pthread_mutex_t mutex;

void insert(int key, void *value) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    if (max_size == 0) {
        records = malloc(sizeof(Record));
    } else {
        records = realloc(records, (max_size + 1) * sizeof(Record));
    }

    records[max_size].key = key;
    records[max_size].value = value;

    max_size++;

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}

void remove(int key) {
    int index = -1;

    pthread_mutex_lock(&mutex);

    for (int i = 0; i < max_size; i++) {
        if (records[i].key == key) {
            index = i;
            break;
        }
    }

    if (index != -1) {
        records[index] = records[max_size - 1];
        max_size--;
    }

    pthread_mutex_unlock(&mutex);
}
```
This implementation is much simpler and easier to understand. It also handles the case where `max_size` is smaller than the current size of the array, which can lead to memory leaks or data corruption.

In this example, we use a single mutex object `mutex` to protect both the reallocation of the arrays and the insertion/removal of elements in the array. This ensures that concurrent access to the array is properly synchronized, avoiding race conditions and data corruption.

Note that the `pthread_mutex_lock()` function is used to lock the mutex object before accessing the shared data, and the `pthread_mutex_unlock()` function is used to unlock it after access. This ensures that the critical section of code is properly protected from concurrent access, avoiding race conditions and data corruption.

Also note that the `insert()` and `remove()` functions are not thread-safe by themselves. They must be called within a critical section that is locked using the mutex object to ensure proper synchronization.']

