    char *uppercased_string = malloc(length + 1);
    if (!uppercased_string) {
        printf("Error: Failed to allocate memory for uppercased string\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Convert the input string to uppercase
    for (size_t i = 0; i < length; i++) {
        uppercased_string[i] = toupper(string[i]);
    }
    uppercased_string[length] = '\0';

    // Calculate the SHA-256 hash of the uppercased string using a standard library function
    unsigned char *hash = malloc(SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH);
    if (!hash) {
        printf("Error: Failed to allocate memory for hash\n");
        free(uppercased_string);
        return NULL;
    }
    SHA256(uppercased_string, length, hash);

    // Free the memory allocated for the uppercased string
    free(uppercased_string);

    // Convert the hash to a hexadecimal string and return it
    size_t hash_length = SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH * 2 + 1;
    char *hex_hash = malloc(hash_length);
    if (!hex_hash) {
        printf("Error: Failed to allocate memory for hex hash\n");
        free(hash);
        return NULL;
    }
    for (size_t i = 0; i < SHA256_DIGEST_LENGTH; i++) {
        sprintf(hex_hash + (i * 2), "%02x", hash[i]);
    }
    free(hash);
    return hex_hash;
}
```
The above code defines a function `generate_data` that dynamically allocates memory for and initializes two arrays: one for double precision floating-point numbers and one for char strings. It then allocates memory for and initializes an array of data_point structures, where each structure contains a double value and a char string. The data points are initialized with random values within a specified range and unique string identifiers.

The function also defines another function `sha256_hash` that converts a string to uppercase and returns its SHA-256 hash using a standard library function. It handles any exceptions gracefully, ensuring no memory leaks or performance impacts. The function is modular and maintainable, allowing for easy updates or modifications in the future.

