### Decryption Functions

The decryption functions are similar to the encryption functions but they use different padding schemes and algorithms. The `decryptString` function uses the `AES-CBC` algorithm with PKCS7 padding for decryption and the `decryptBuffer` function uses the `AES-GCM` algorithm for decryption.

```c
// Decrypt a string using AES-CBC and PKCS7 padding
void decryptString(const char* ciphertext, const char* password, char** plaintext) {
  // Get the IV for CBC mode encryption
  unsigned char* iv = malloc(16 * sizeof(unsigned char));
  memcpy(iv, ciphertext, 16);

  // Decrypt the ciphertext string using AES-CBC with PKCS7 padding
  EVP_CIPHER_CTX* ctx;
  int len = strlen(ciphertext) - 16;
  unsigned char* plain = malloc((len + 1) * sizeof(unsigned char));
  size_t plainLen, blockSize;

  // Initialize the decryption context
  EVP_DecryptInit(&ctx, EVP_aes_256_cbc(), NULL, password);
  EVP_CIPHER_CTX_set_padding(ctx, EVP_CIPH_PKCS7_PADDING);

  // Decrypt the ciphertext string and get the plaintext length
  EVP_DecryptUpdate(&ctx, plain, &plainLen, (unsigned char*)ciphertext + 16, len);

  // Get the block size of the decryption algorithm
  blockSize = EVP_CIPHER_CTX_block_size(ctx);

  // Unpad the plaintext string with PKCS7 padding
  unsigned char* unpaddedPlaintext = malloc((plainLen + blockSize) * sizeof(unsigned char));
  memset(unpaddedPlaintext, 0x10, plainLen);
  memcpy(unpaddedPlaintext + plainLen, (unsigned char*)plain, plainLen);

  // Decrypt the unpadded plaintext string and get the final plaintext length
  EVP_DecryptUpdate(&ctx, plain, &plainLen, unpaddedPlaintext, plainLen + blockSize);

  // Free the decryption context and other memory allocations
  EVP_DecryptFree(&ctx);
  free(unpaddedPlaintext);

  // Return the plaintext string as a malloc'd buffer
  *plaintext = malloc((plainLen + 1) * sizeof(unsigned char));
  memcpy(*plaintext, (char*)plain, plainLen);
}

// Decrypt a buffer using AES-GCM
void decryptBuffer(const unsigned char* ciphertext, const size_t cipherLen, const char* password, unsigned char** plaintext) {
  // Initialize the decryption context
  EVP_CIPHER_CTX* ctx;
  int len = (int)cipherLen;

  // Get the IV for GCM mode encryption
  unsigned char* iv = malloc(16 * sizeof(unsigned char));
  memcpy(iv, ciphertext, 16);

  // Decrypt the ciphertext buffer using AES-GCM
  EVP_DecryptInit(&ctx, EVP_aes_256_gcm(), NULL, password);
  EVP_CIPHER_CTX_set_padding(ctx, EVP_CIPH_NO_PADDING);

  // Decrypt the ciphertext buffer and get the plaintext length
  EVP_DecryptUpdate(&ctx, *plaintext, &len, (unsigned char*)ciphertext + 16, len - 16);

  // Free the decryption context and other memory allocations
  EVP_DecryptFree(&ctx);
  free(iv);
}
```

### Main Functions

The main functions are used to demonstrate the usage of the encryption and decryption functions. The `main` function calls the `encryptString` and `decryptString` functions to encrypt and decrypt a string using AES-CBC and PKCS7 padding, respectively. The `main` function also demonstrates how to use the `encryptBuffer` and `decryptBuffer` functions to encrypt and decrypt a buffer using AES-GCM.

```c
int main(void) {
  // Test encryption and decryption of strings with AES-CBC and PKCS7 padding
  char* plaintext = "Hello, World!";
  char* password = "my_secret_password";
  char* ciphertext;

  encryptString(plaintext, password, &ciphertext);
  printf("Encrypted string: %s\n", ciphertext);

  decryptString(ciphertext, password, &plaintext);
  printf("Decrypted string: %s\n", plaintext);

  // Test encryption and decryption of buffers with AES-GCM
  unsigned char* buffer = (unsigned char*)malloc(sizeof(unsigned char) * 16);
  size_t cipherLen;
  encryptBuffer(buffer, 16, password, &ciphertext, &cipherLen);
  printf("Encrypted buffer: ");
  for (int i = 0; i < cipherLen; i++) {
    printf("%02x", ciphertext[i]);
  }
  printf("\n");

  decryptBuffer(ciphertext, cipherLen, password, &buffer);
  printf("Decrypted buffer: ");
  for (int i = 0; i < 16; i++) {
    printf("%02x", buffer[i]);
  }
  printf("\n");

  return 0;
}
```

